{"id":"0ceaa2cf-e8e6-4478-9703-0365dd6830b7","object":"chat.completion","created":1785029779,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    edges = case['edges']\\n    refinement = case['refinement']\\n    groups = set(refinement.values())\\n    members = {g: sorted([n for n, grp in refinement.items() if grp == g]) for g in groups}\\n    agg = {}\\n    for u, v, w in edges:\\n        gu = refinement[u]\\n        gv = refinement[v]\\n        pair = tuple(sorted([gu, gv]))\\n        agg[pair] = agg.get(pair, 0) + w\\n    agg_edges = sorted([[g1, g2, w] for (g1, g2), w in agg.items()], key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\\n    return {'aggregate_edges': agg_edges, 'members': members}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function solve(case) that processes the given case payload. The payload contains \"edges\" (list of [source, target, weight]) and \"refinement\" (dictionary mapping node to group). We need to produce \"aggregate_edges\" and \"members\".\n\nBased on the schema: \"members\" is an object with dynamic keys from case.refinement.values() (the group names). Each key maps to an array of strings (the nodes belonging to that group). The \"aggregate_edges\" is an array of arrays, each of length 3: [group1, group2, weight]. The edges should be aggregated: for each edge (u,v,w), find group of u and group of v, then add weight to that group pair. Since it's undirected, we canonicalize order (alphabetical? The schema says \"Canonicalize each aggregate endpoint pair so undirected duplicates coincide.\" Typically sort the pair. Also include self-loops (if same group). Then sort aggregate edges? The description says \"Return sorted aggregate edges and sorted membership lists.\" So sort the list of edges and sort the membership arrays.\n\nNeed to ensure that each original node maps to exactly one refined community (given by refinement dict). So no missing nodes.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Extract edges and refinement from case.\n2. Build mapping from node to group.\n3. Collect all groups from refinement values (set).\n4. Build members dict: for each group, list of nodes that map to it (sorted).\n5. Aggregate edges: for each edge (u,v,w):\n   - gu = refinement[u]; gv = refinement[v]\n   - Canonicalize pair: ensure (min(gu,gv), max(gu,gv)) in some order? But groups are strings, so we sort alphabetically? The schema doesn't specify ordering, but \"Canonicalize each aggregate endpoint pair so undirected duplicates coincide\" means we need a consistent ordering. Since groups are strings, we can use sorted tuple.\n   - Add weight to that pair (using dictionary).\n6. Convert aggregate edges dict to list of [g1, g2, w], then sort by (g1, g2) maybe? The description says \"sorted aggregate edges\". We can sort by first group then second group (alphabetically). \n7. Return {\"aggregate_edges\": sorted_aggregate_edges, \"members\": members}.\n\nMake sure to not mutate case. Use standard libs only.\n\nThe output must exactly match the schema: members object with dynamic keys from refinement.values(). So we must include all groups even if some have no nodes? The refinement mapping defines node->group, all nodes are mapped, so each group will have at least one node. So fine.\n\nWe need to return a JSON-serializable object. So dictionary with lists.\n\nLet's write the Python code. Enclose in JSON string for the \"implementation\" key.\n\nImportant: The solve(case) function signature. The case is the payload object (dictionary). It has no outer keys like case_id, seed. So we just use case[\"edges\"] and case[\"refinement\"].\n\nWe should also handle possible missing fields? But the fixture shows edges and refinement present. We'll assume they are present.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON with \"implementation\" key containing the escaped Python source.\n\nWrite code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    edges = case[\"edges\"]\n    refinement = case[\"refinement\"]\n    # Build groups\n    groups = set(refinement.values())\n    # Build members\n    members = {g: sorted([node for node, grp in refinement.items() if grp == g]) for g in groups}\n    # Aggregate edges\n    agg = {}\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        gu = refinement[u]\n        gv = refinement[v]\n        # Canonicalize: sort pair\n        pair = tuple(sorted([gu, gv]))\n        agg[pair] = agg.get(pair, 0) + w\n    # Convert to list of lists and sort\n    agg_edges = sorted([[g1, g2, w] for (g1, g2), w in agg.items()], key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n    return {\"aggregate_edges\": agg_edges, \"members\": members}\n\nBut note: The member lists should be sorted. Yes.\n\nPotential issue: The output schema for members says \"items\": { \"type\": \"string\" } but we are using list of strings. That's fine.\n\nAlso ensure we return exactly those two keys.\n\nNow, encode as JSON string for the implementation. Use json.dumps on the source code (as string) then put into object.\n\nBut the instruction: \"Your entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with the single key \"implementation\". The first non-whitespace character must be { and the last non-whitespace character must be }. Do not use Markdown code fences. Do not add a preamble, explanation, or trailing text. Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\"\n\nSo we need to output something like: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nWe should carefully escape newlines and quotes. Use json.dumps to generate the string, then construct final JSON.\n\nLet's do it programmatically in thought, then write final output.\n\nWe'll produce the Python source as a string, then call json.dumps on it, then put into {\"implementation\": that_dumps_result}.\n\nAlternatively, we can manually escape. Better to use json.dumps to be safe.\n\nBut we are writing the response directly, so we need to output the JSON text. We'll use triple quotes for the source? No, just a normal string.\n\nI'll write the source as:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    edges = case['edges']\n    refinement = case['refinement']\n    groups = set(refinement.values())\n    members = {g: sorted([n for n, grp in refinement.items() if grp == g]) for g in groups}\n    agg = {}\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        gu = refinement[u]\n        gv = refinement[v]\n        pair = tuple(sorted([gu, gv]))\n        agg[pair] = agg.get(pair, 0) + w\n    agg_edges = sorted([[g1, g2, w] for (g1, g2), w in agg.items()], key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n    return {'aggregate_edges': agg_edges, 'members': members}\n\nNow escape quotes: The source uses single quotes inside, but we'll wrap in double quotes for JSON string, so need to escape internal double quotes if any (none). Also escape backslashes (none) and newlines as \\n.\n\nWe'll use Python json.dumps to escape properly.\n\nLet's simulate: \nsource = \"def solve(case):\\n    edges = case['edges']\\n    refinement = case['refinement']\\n    groups = set(refinement.values())\\n    members = {g: sorted([n for n, grp in refinement.items() if grp == g]) for g in groups}\\n    agg = {}\\n    for u, v, w in edges:\\n        gu = refinement[u]\\n        gv = refinement[v]\\n        pair = tuple(sorted([gu, gv]))\\n        agg[pair] = agg.get(pair, 0) + w\\n    agg_edges = sorted([[g1, g2, w] for (g1, g2), w in agg.items()], key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\\n    return {'aggregate_edges': agg_edges, 'members': members}\"\n\nThen json.dumps(source) gives a string with proper escaping. Then final JSON: {\"implementation\": <escaped string>}\n\nBut note: In the final response, we must output that JSON string exactly. No extra spaces? The instruction says \"first non-whitespace character must be { and last non-whitespace character must be }\". So we can have whitespace inside as long as it's within JSON. We'll output compact JSON without extra spaces to be safe.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":